  "narrative": {
    "top_frame": "Randy V\u00e1squez shut down Arizona's bats for six innings, and the Padres offense was patient enough to make it matter.",
    "what_this_means": "This was not a comeback. This was a team that got ahead early and never let go. V\u00e1squez allowed two hits through six scoreless innings while striking out one. A dominant stretch that kept Arizona's lineup honest. The Padres scored in four of the first two innings, building a lead that felt comfortable even when the bullpen stumbled. Manny Machado drove in four runs on a 3-for-5 night, and Jackson Merrill added two more hits and two RBI, but this was a balanced offensive performance. 16 hits spread across the lineup. The margin held because the pitching held first. When Germ\u00e1n M\u00e1rquez gave up four runs in the seventh, the Padres were already up 6\u20130. The game was decided by the second inning, and the rest was execution. They didn't need a late swing. They didn't need drama. They needed a clean start and a starter who delivered it.",
